<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Populonia</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 280 BCE (approx.) · capital Populonia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The only major Etruscan city on the sea, and the iron-smelting centre of ancient Italy, working ore shipped from the island of Elba.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Populonia, Etruscan Pupluna or Fufluna, stood on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Piombino</a> promontory facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>. Villanovan settlements appeared there in the 9th century BCE, and by the 7th century princely tumulus tombs in the San Cerbone necropolis show a powerful elite. Ancient writers disagreed over whether it was founded by Corsicans or by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/volaterrae">Volaterrae</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Populonia's economy rested on metals: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from the Campigliese and, from the 6th century BCE, iron ore from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> smelted in furnaces around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-baratti">Gulf of Baratti</a>, whose slag heaps later buried the tombs. It was among the first Etruscan cities to mint coins, in the 5th century BCE, often showing the smith god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sethlans">Sethlans</a> or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgon">Gorgon</a> head, and its port linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corsican-republic">Corsica</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Populonia became a Roman ally in the 3rd century BCE and in 205 BCE supplied iron for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a>'s fleet against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> during the civil wars of the 80s BCE, it declined; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> found it largely deserted. The slag heaps were mined again for iron in the 20th century, uncovering the necropolis.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/populonia.jpg" alt="Populonia" width="320" height="172"><figcaption>Image: PROPOLI87 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parco_archeologico_visto_dall%27alto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 280 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Populonia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pupluna, Fufluna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-540&amp;focus=populonia" aria-label="Populonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4933 651 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Populonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5253 907L5290 918M5253 907L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-540&amp;focus=populonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

